a3e8b6e3-e359-4066-94ef-329d1b0625a6&stpe=3Ddefault>=0A=0A=0A=0AReverse acq=
ui-hire=0A=0AIn recent years, the US technology industry invented a new kin=
d of =0Amergers-and-acquisitions transaction. Traditionally there were two =
main sorts =0Aof M&A transaction:=0A=0A * The acquirer buys the target comp=
any, in a merger or stock purchase; the =0Ashareholders of the target compa=
ny get something (cash, acquirer stock) in =0Aexchange for their shares of =
the target, and the acquirer ends up owning the =0Atarget corporate entity.=
 =0A * The acquirer buys the assets of the company, in an asset purchase; t=
he =0Atarget company gets something (cash, acquirer stock) in exchange for =
its =0Abusiness. If the target is sellingall of its assets, it will be left=
 as an =0Aempty shell, and will probably distribute the cash or stock to it=
s shareholders =0Aand liquidate. =0AThese are both pretty normal transactio=
ns, and many rules are set up to treat =0Athem similarly. So, for instance,=
 Delaware corporate law requires a shareholder =0Avote for amerger <https:/=
/bloom.bg/3VkzUgH>, or for a sale =0A<https://bloom.bg/4hEotJv> of =E2=80=
=9Call or substantially all=E2=80=9D of a company=E2=80=99s assets. =0AUSan=
titrust law <https://bloom.bg/4AQNcSd> requires <https://bloom.bg/4AH2dG6> =
=0Aa =E2=80=9CHart-Scott-Rodino premerger notification report=E2=80=9D =E2=
=80=94 where the acquirertells =0Aantitrust regulators about the deal <http=
s://bloom.bg/4ALSFtD> and gives them =0Athe opportunity to object =E2=80=94=
 for any acquisition of more than$133.9 million worth=0A <https://bloom.bg/=
47Alo74> of stock or assets. You might get different =0Aaccounting or tax t=
reatment for different deal structures, but many of the =0Abig-picture effe=
cts are the same however you structure the deal.=0A=0AThe antitrust part is=
 important. The big tech companies are very big, there is =0Aa lot of polit=
ical and regulatory worry that they are monopolists, and there is =0Aat lea=
st some winner-takes-all flavor to the AI race. Meta or Alphabet or =0ANvid=
ia might really want to do a $20 billion AI acquisition here or there, but =
=0Athat might be risky from an antitrust perspective. So they=E2=80=99re in=
 the market for =0Aways to do acquisitions without, technically, doing acqu=
isitions.=0A=0AMany early-stage artificial intelligence startups consist mo=
stly of (1) people =0Aand (2) ideas. The startup has some AI researchers, a=
nd they=E2=80=99ve come up with =0Asome model or harness or chip design or =
robot or whatever, and it=E2=80=99s pretty =0Apromising. But the startup do=
esn=E2=80=99t have, like, a giant portfolio of data-center =0Areal estate, =
or a robot assembly line, or a bunch of customer contracts.=0A=0AIf a big t=
ech company wants to acquire a startup like that, it could buy it in =0Aa m=
erger, or buy all of its assets. But the new structure is:=0A=0A * The acqu=
irer hires the target=E2=80=99s employees, or most of them, or at least the=
 =0Apeople who are critical to its valuable ideas. =0A * The acquirer gets =
a waiver, from the target=E2=80=99s board of directors, allowing =0Ait to d=
o all that hiring. (California doesn=E2=80=99t enforce non-compete agreemen=
ts, =0Abut if you are going to, like, have the chief executive officer of t=
he target =0Aemail all of the target=E2=80=99s employees from her work emai=
l saying =E2=80=9Chey come with =0Ame to the acquirer; we=E2=80=99ll match =
your salary,=E2=80=9D then it=E2=80=99s probably good to get =0Athe target=
=E2=80=99s board=E2=80=99s permission.) =0A * The acquirer licenses the tar=
get=E2=80=99s intellectual property: The target enters =0Ainto a nonexclusi=
ve license allowing the acquirer to use the target=E2=80=99s ideas, =0Apate=
nts, chip designs, etc., for its own purposes. =0A * In exchange for the wa=
iver (Step 2) and license (Step 3), the acquirer pays =0Athe target a big p=
ile of cash. =0A * The target is now more or less an empty shell. It has lo=
st most of its =0Aemployees, or at least the ones who know about the stuff =
that made it valuable. =0AIt still technically owns its intellectual proper=
ty =E2=80=94 it gave the acquirer a =0Anon-exclusive license =E2=80=94 but =
everyone who knows anything about that intellectual =0Aproperty has decampe=
d for the acquirer, so it can=E2=80=99t do anything useful with it. =0AIt=
=E2=80=99s just a collection of miscellany, plus the big pile of cash that =
it got =0Afrom the acquirer. =0A * So the target pays out the cash to its e=
mployees. Then maybe it shuts down, =0Aor maybe it keeps going in some less=
 ambitious form. =0AThis structure is called a =E2=80=9Creverse acqui-hire.=
=E2=80=9D [1]  <>=0A=0AThis structure has some disadvantages, compared to a=
 traditional merger. It is =0Anot especially tax-efficient; the target pays=
 tax on the licensing fee. [2]  <> =0ABecause it leaves the target as an in=
dependent company, and gives the acquirer =0Aonly a nonexclusive license, i=
t is not quite as good as a merger from a =0Acompetitive perspective: What =
if your biggest competitor manages to strike its =0Aown licensing deal with=
 the stub target company=3F=0A=0ABut it has some pretty important advantage=
s. For one thing, it probably =0Adoesn=E2=80=99t require a vote of the targ=
et=E2=80=99s shareholders. (Just the board of =0Adirectors.) This allows it=
 to happen more quickly than a merger. Itmight also =0Aallow the reverse ac=
qui-hire to happen over the objections of a majority of =0Ashareholders, th=
ough that=E2=80=99s probably not a big factor. (The big shareholders of =0A=
an early-stage AI startup will probably be mostly (1) venture capitalists w=
ho =0Aare represented on the board and (2) co-founders and other employees =
who have =0Asome leverage over getting acqui-hired.) But in theory, a rever=
se acqui-hire =0Athat is good for the employees and bad for the outside sha=
reholders might be =0Adoable, whereas a merger that is good for employees a=
nd bad for outside =0Ashareholders would be harder to pull off. [3]  <>=0A=
=0AThe main advantage, though, is probably antitrust. If it=E2=80=99s not a=
 stock or =0Aasset acquisition, it doesn=E2=80=99t require an HSR filing, w=
hich means you can close =0Athe deal before the antitrust regulators can do=
 anything to stop it. Ionce =0Aexplained this deal structure <https://bloom=
.bg/4y1EGNu> with a thought =0Aexperiment: =E2=80=9CImagine if mergers and=
 acquisitions were illegal.=E2=80=9D The reverse =0Aaqui-hire is a way to d=
o a merger without the legal trouble of antitrust review.=0A=0AThis is all =
pretty controversial, for more or less the reasons that you=E2=80=99d =0Aex=
pect. If you think of a reverse acqui-hire as a deal structure that was =0A=
invented to avoid antitrust scrutiny, then it will obviously annoy people w=
ho =0Awant more antitrust scrutiny. Bloomberg Newsreported earlier this yea=
r =0A<https://bloom.bg/3W7yciQ>:=0A=0AThe US Federal Trade Commission is ta=
king a closer look at moves by major tech =0Acompanies to hire away the emp=
loyees of a startup instead of buying the venture =0Aoutright, the agency=
=E2=80=99s Chairman Andrew Ferguson said, addressing a growing =0Apractice =
seen as a way to avoid antitrust review.=0A=0A=E2=80=9CWe are beginning to =
examine these acqui-hires to make sure they are not an =0Aattempt to get ar=
ound=E2=80=9D the agency=E2=80=99s merger review process, Ferguson said ...=
 in =0Aan interview on Bloomberg Television.=0A=0AAnd the New York Times re=
ported last month <https://bloom.bg/4dl3d91>:=0A=0AThe Justice Department i=
s investigating whether Nvidia tried to skirt =0Aantitrust scrutiny of a de=
al it struck with the artificial intelligence =0Achipmaker Groq last year, =
according to two people with knowledge of the inquiry.=0A=0AGroq described =
the deal at the time as a =E2=80=9Cnonexclusive licensing agreement=E2=80=
=9D =0Athat gave Nvidia access to Groq=E2=80=99s chips, which are custom-bu=
ilt for running =0AA.I. As part of the arrangement, Groq=E2=80=99s chief ex=
ecutive, Jonathan Ross, and its =0Achief operating officer, Sunny Madra, jo=
ined Nvidia. ...=0A=0AGovernment officials have considered whether these de=
als should receive more =0Ascrutiny while they grapple with the way a small=
 number of companies have =0Aconsolidated power over A.I.=0A=0AConceptually=
, if reverse acqui-hires are another sort of M&A transaction, then =0Atheys=
hould be covered by antitrust law. There are some line-drawing problems =0A=
there =E2=80=94 justhiring someone probably shouldn=E2=80=99t trigger antit=
rust review =E2=80=94 but =0Ayou can see why the regulators are interested.=
=0A=0ASimilarly, though, if reverse acqui-hires are another sort of M&A tra=
nsaction, =0Athen they should be treated that way undercorporate law. A mer=
ger, or a sale of =0Aall of a company=E2=80=99s assets, triggers certain co=
rporate requirements: Directors =0Ahave a fiduciary duty to maximize value =
in a sale, [4]  <> and the shareholders =0Ahave to vote to approve the deal=
. If a reverse acqui-hire effectively means =0Aselling all of a company=E2=
=80=99s assets for cash =E2=80=94 because its assets are basically =0Aits p=
eople and ideas =E2=80=94 then shouldn=E2=80=99t that trigger the same requ=
irements=3F=0A=0AThe Financial Times reports <https://bloom.bg/4hIARGW>:=0A=
=0ANvidia=E2=80=99s $20bn licensing deal with Groq fleeced employees who we=
re left out of =0Athe transaction, according to a lawsuit filed by two engi=
neers who owned shares =0Ain the start-up.=0A=0AThe legal complaint filed o=
n Friday in a Delaware corporate law court alleges =0Athat Groq=E2=80=99s b=
oard improperly sold the company=E2=80=99s core assets and top employees =
=0Ato the $5tn tech titan in 2025, consigning those left behind to a hollow=
ed-out =0Ashell.=0A=0AThe transaction was framed by Nvidia at the time as a=
 =E2=80=9Cnon-exclusive=E2=80=9D =0Alicensing arrangement, with Groq remain=
ing as an independent and functioning =0Acompany.=0A=0ABut, according to th=
e lawsuit, the Groq board was riven with conflicts of =0Ainterest and faile=
d to observe its legal obligation to get the best price and =0Astructure fo=
r all shareholders, not allowing some of them to vote on the =0Atransaction=
.=0A=0AHere is the complaint <https://bloom.bg/4i3Miub>, whose basic point=
 is =E2=80=9Coh =0Acome on this is a merger=E2=80=9D:=0A=0AGroq=E2=80=99s b=
oard of directors (the =E2=80=9CBoard=E2=80=9D) sold the Company to NVIDIA =
without the =0Astockholder vote Delaware law requires and without any proce=
ss designed to test =0Aor maximize the value of what NVIDIA bought. A major=
ity of the Board was =0Aconflicted. It handed NVIDIA Groq=E2=80=99s valuabl=
e technology and the engineers who =0Abuilt Groq, took billions of dollars =
in benefits for itself, senior management, =0Aand affiliated funds that it =
did not share with Groq=E2=80=99s other stockholders, and =0Athen squeezed =
out those stockholders at a lowball price it set itself. =E2=80=A6=0A=0ABut=
 a merger that avoids antitrust rules:=0A=0AA stock or asset acquisition wa=
s unattractive because, given NVIDIA=E2=80=99s market =0Ashare, a Groq merg=
er would have faced serious antitrust scrutiny. =E2=80=A6=0A=0ABecause the =
buyer acquires neither voting securities nor assets, the =0Atransaction fal=
ls outside the federal antitrust regulators=E2=80=99 premerger =0Anotificat=
ion regime. ... The structure has become increasingly common in recent =0Ay=
ears, promoted by venture investors and savvy transactional attorneys. =E2=
=80=A6=0A=0ANVIDIA had reason to prize that avoidance. A prior attempt to a=
cquire Arm =0AHoldings from SoftBank drew sustained multi-jurisdictional an=
titrust scrutiny =0Aand NVIDIA ultimately abandoned the transaction without=
 closing. An acquirer =0Athat has watched a headline acquisition of a semic=
onductor licensor collapse =0Aunder regulatory pressure has a powerful inst=
itutional incentive to design its =0Anext acquisition so that no filing is =
required.=0A=0AThe complaint also argues that, while technically the licens=
e to Nvidia was =0Anon-exclusive and Groq continued as an independent compa=
ny, in practice there =0Awas nothing left:=0A=0AThe supposedly =E2=80=9Cnon=
-exclusive=E2=80=9D license grant was substantively an asset =0Atransfer. =
=E2=80=A6=0A=0ANo rational third-party licensee will pay significant value =
for a =0Anon-exclusive right to technology that the world=E2=80=99s dominan=
t AI chip company =0Aalready holds on the same or broader terms ... and tha=
t it is updating daily =0Awith the very workforce that created the underlyi=
ng intellectual property. The =0Acanvass of 36 parties produced no compelli=
ng bid [for the remainder of the =0Acompany] for that reason: once Groq=E2=
=80=99s Board agreed to let NVIDIA develop the =0Aassets, whatever interest=
 Groq retained in them ceased to have independent =0Amarket value.=0A=0AThi=
s all seems about right to me, but I=E2=80=99m not sure where it gets you. =
Part of =0Athe argument here is that the reverse acqui-hire is a novel form=
 of M&A that =0Ashould be treated like other forms of M&A, and should have =
to be fair to =0Ashareholders.=0A=0ABut the other part of the argument is t=
hat this deal wasn=E2=80=99t fair to =0Ashareholders, and I=E2=80=99m not s=
ure. Groq=E2=80=99s shareholders =E2=80=94 including the plaintiffs =0Ain t=
his lawsuit =E2=80=94 weren=E2=80=99tzeroed in the transaction. Nvidia paid=
 $17 billion to =0AGroq, in cash, for the license, and most of that money h=
as been paid out to =0Ashareholders. [5]  <> Their complaint is that other =
shareholders =E2=80=94 the founders =0Aand employees who went to Nvidia =E2=
=80=94 got more. The headline value of the Nvidia =0Aacqui-hirewas $20 bill=
ion <https://bloom.bg/4y3Dqtg>, that is, the $17 billion =0Apaid to Groq pl=
us $3 billion paid to employees for joining Nvidia. The lawsuit =0Aobjects:=
=0A=0A[Jonathan] Ross=E2=80=99s position illustrates the conflict. He was s=
imultaneously a =0AGroq stockholder, Groq=E2=80=99s Chief Executive Officer=
, a Groq director, the =0Aprincipal negotiator of the Company=E2=80=99s sid=
e of the License, and the individual =0ANVIDIA most wanted to hire. He rece=
ived NVIDIA equity compensation from the $3 =0Abillion pool on top of the p=
roceeds of his Groq ownership. An ordinary common =0Astockholder could mone=
tize the value surrendered to NVIDIA only through the =0Aconsideration allo=
cated to her shares; she could not take a discount on those =0Ashares and b=
e paid separately for following the technology to NVIDIA. Ross =0Acould, an=
d did.=0A=0AYeah but that=E2=80=99s true in any merger where the CEO keeps =
his job. The value of =0AGroq was largely in its employees, and Groq=E2=80=
=99s shareholders cleared $17 billion =0Aby selling the rights to those emp=
loyees to Nvidia. Of course the employees =0Aalso got paid.=0A=0A =0A<https=
://sli.bloomberg.com/click=3Fs=3D868432&stpe=3Ddefault&li=3D20221520&m=3Dbb=
4a280bebb1586390b9615b8886024a&p=3D10052026181206&lctg=3Da3e8b6e3-e359-4066=
-94ef-329d1b0625a6>=0A=0A =0A<https://sli.bloomberg.com/click=3Fs=3D868432&=
stpe=3Ddefault&li=3D20221520&m=3Dbb4a280bebb1586390b9615b8886024a&p=3D10052=
026181206&lctg=3Da3e8b6e3-e359-4066-94ef-329d1b0625a6>=0A=0AAnthropic chari=
table giving=0A=0AFrontier artificial intelligence labs are locked in bitte=
r competition for the =0Abest researchers: The lab with the best researcher=
s will build the best model =0Aand make a zillion dollars; the lab with the=
 second-best researchers might =0Afail. It is critical, for normal sharehol=
der-value-creation reasons as well as =0Amore fancifulsave/destroy-the-worl=
d <https://bloom.bg/4dgPgsI> reasons, to hire =0Athe best researchers. And =
so this competition largely takes the normal form: =0ABig AI labs pay their=
 researchers lots of money.=0A=0ABut that=E2=80=99s not the only form it ca=
n take. There seems to be a  strong =0Aempirical correlation <https://bloom=
.bg/4y3cava> between (1) being a good AI =0Aresearcher and (2) being inter=
ested ineffective altruism =0A<https://bloom.bg/4AQvNJe>. (You might wonder=
 if this is a historical accident: =0AEarly AI research was an obscure subc=
ulture with a lot of overlap with EA, but =0Anow that =E2=80=9CAI researche=
r=E2=80=9D is a highly legible and lucrative career path, maybe =0Athe corr=
elation will weaken. Or maybe not; maybe there is some inherent =0Aconnecti=
on between being good at AI and having EA views.)=0A=0AAnd so a lot of rese=
archers at top AI labs have some =E2=80=9Cearning to give =0A<https://bloom=
.bg/3VtOCSy>=E2=80=9D tendencies, donating large portions of their =0Amass=
ive paychecks to charities. (Because they are EA-oriented, many of them =0A=
give to charities whose mission is to stop their employers from building =
=0Asuperintelligent AI.Is that =E2=80=A6 effective <https://bloom.bg/4AV86Q=
m>=3F) =0A=0AIf you ran an AI lab, you might design your compensation pract=
ices around =0Athese facts. You might recruit researchers by offering to pa=
y them a =0Acompetitive headline number =E2=80=94 like, $20 million a year =
=E2=80=94 but split into (1) $5 =0Amillion paid directly to them and (2) $1=
5 million paid directly to the charity =0Aof their choice. This compensatio=
n design has several key advantages:=0A=0A * It will appeal to EA types, an=
d a lot of good AI researchers are EA types. =0AYou will maximize sharehold=
er value by recruiting the best AI researchers. =0A(Again, perhaps this lin=
k is weakening over time.) =0A * If you=E2=80=99re the founder of an AI lab=
, you might be an EA type yourself, and =0Ayou might prefer to hire employe=
es who funnel their large paychecks to charity =0Arather than to yachts. =
=0A * Relatedly, we often <https://bloom.bg/4hH831D> discuss =0A<https://bl=
oom.bg/4hNk5Xr> the <https://bloom.bg/4AOusTf> fact =0A<https://bloom.bg/4r=
LwUGh> that the competitive salary for many AI researchers =0Ais enough to =
retire on immediately. You do not want to hire researchers who =0Aretire im=
mediately. You want them to keep working. Making sure that most of =0Atheir=
 paycheck goes to charity, rather than to savings, is helpful for =0Aretent=
ion and thus maximizes shareholder value. =0A * If you are paying the chari=
table donations directly, rather than through =0Athe employees, it is possi=
ble that (1) you can refuse to direct the donations =0Ato Stop AI Labs From=
 Doing AI charities and/or (2) the Stop AI Labs From Doing =0AAI charities =
will refuse to take donations from you, which is maybe also =0Ahelpful for =
shareholder value. [6]  <> =0AAt the Information, Cory Weinberg reports <ht=
tps://bloom.bg/47B7QrZ> that =0AAnthropic does in fact have more or less th=
is compensation structure, which =0Amakes for slightly unintuitive accounti=
ng and disclosure:=0A=0AWhen Anthropic shared financial figures recently wi=
th prospective investors in =0Aits planned initial public offering, it incl=
uded a surprising glimpse of the =0Aenormous riches about to hit nonprofits=
 and philanthropies=E2=80=94and the cost to =0Ashareholders.=0A=0AThe compa=
ny reported a non-cash expense of more than $660 million for stock =0Aused =
to match employees=E2=80=99 charitable contributions in the six months from=
 =0AOctober 2025 to March 2026, investors said. That expense, which is alre=
ady =0Aunusually large, will likely balloon into the billions of dollars in=
 the coming =0Aquarters, after an IPO expected to create what could be an u=
nprecedented wave =0Aof corporate donations.=0A=0AThe charges are tied to s=
tock awards to employees that they pledge to =0Acharities that Anthropic ma=
tches with stock of its own. Anthropic executives =0Ahave said the unusuall=
y generous matching program is an important recruiting =0Atool, and they ha=
ve emphasized how it underlines its public benefit mission. =0AAnd it has b=
ecome particularly valuable because of Anthropic=E2=80=99s rapidly rising =
=0Aprivate stock value. =E2=80=A6=0A=0AAnthropic excluded the expense, alon=
g with stock-based compensation and a =0Alarge legal settlement, from its a=
djusted operating profit for the periods. =0ACompanies typically present in=
vestors with adjusted profit metrics that exclude =0Aexpenses they don=E2=
=80=99t pay in cash or aren=E2=80=99t likely to recur.=0A=0AThe charitable =
expense that Anthropic excluded was far larger than any large =0Apublicly t=
raded firm that excluded a similar expense from their adjusted profit =0Ame=
trics. =E2=80=9CThe adjustment for charitable contributions is not common a=
t all,=E2=80=9D =0Asaid Calcbench=E2=80=99s Ghai.=0A=0ANo, right, of course=
, most companies do not report adjusted earnings that =0Aadjust out non-cas=
h charitable giving, because =E2=80=9Cnon-cash charitable giving=E2=80=9D i=
s =0Anot a big thing at most companies. But tech companies oftendo adjust o=
ut =0Astock-basedemployee compensation, [7]  <> and that=E2=80=99s obviousl=
y what=E2=80=99s going on =0Ahere. It=E2=80=99s just that the way Anthropic=
 compensates employees is through =0Acharitable giving.=0A=0AAlso, here=E2=
=80=99s evidence that the AI/EA correlation might be weakening over time:=
=0A=0AThe company matched stock earmarked for charities on a 3-to-1 basis f=
or early =0Aemployees, who joined in 2024 or earlier. Employees had the opt=
ion to donate up =0Ato half their stock to charity as part of the program. =
For employees hired more =0Arecently, the company matches donations of up t=
o a quarter of their stock on a =0A1-to-1 basis.=0A=0AIf you joined Anthrop=
ic in 2023, there was a high probability that you cared a =0Alot about funn=
eling your potential AI riches to effective charities. In 2025, =0Aless. Ev=
entually, after the IPO, they=E2=80=99ll probably phase it out.=0A=0A=0AAna=
lyst programs=0A=0ASure fine <https://bloom.bg/4hvV97T>:=0A=0ACitigroup Inc=
. is slashing the time its investment-banking analysts need to =0Await to =
advance to associate level, part of a push by the bank to attract and =0Are=
tain top talent in a fierce job market that=E2=80=99s seen buyout firms poa=
ch =0Aaggressively from Wall Street.=0A=0AThe move will cut the analyst pro=
gram to two years from three for Citi=E2=80=99s =0Ajunior bankers, accelera=
