t for different deal structures, but many of the big-picture effects are th=
e same however you structure the deal.</p> <p style=3D"margin: 16px 0;">The=
 antitrust part is important. The big tech companies are very big,&nbsp;the=
re is a lot of political and regulatory worry that they are monopolists, an=
d there is at least some winner-takes-all flavor to the AI race. Meta or Al=
phabet or Nvidia might really want to do a $20 billion AI acquisition here =
or there, but that might be risky from an antitrust perspective. So they=E2=
=80=99re in the market for ways to do acquisitions without, technically, do=
ing acquisitions.</p> <p style=3D"margin: 16px 0;">Many early-stage artific=
ial intelligence startups consist mostly of (1) people and (2) ideas. The s=
tartup has&nbsp;some AI researchers, and they=E2=80=99ve come up with some =
model or harness or chip design or robot or whatever, and it=E2=80=99s pret=
ty promising. But the startup doesn=E2=80=99t have, like, a giant portfolio=
 of data-center real estate, or a robot assembly line, or a bunch of custom=
er contracts.&nbsp;</p> <p style=3D"margin: 16px 0;">If a big tech company =
wants to acquire a startup like that, it&nbsp;<em>could&nbsp;</em>buy it in=
 a merger, or buy all of its assets. But the new structure is:</p> <ol styl=
e=3D"list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 40px; margin: 16px 0;"> <li st=
yle=3D"margin-bottom: 5px;">The acquirer&nbsp;<em>hires the target=E2=80=99=
s employees,&nbsp;</em>or most of them, or at least the people who are crit=
ical to its valuable ideas.</li> <li style=3D"margin-bottom: 5px;">The acqu=
irer gets a waiver, from the target=E2=80=99s board of directors, allowing =
it to do all that hiring. (California doesn=E2=80=99t enforce non-compete a=
greements, but if you are going to, like, have the chief executive officer =
of the target email all of the target=E2=80=99s employees from her work ema=
il saying =E2=80=9Chey come with me to the acquirer; we=E2=80=99ll match yo=
ur salary,=E2=80=9D then it=E2=80=99s probably good to get the target=E2=80=
=99s board=E2=80=99s permission.)</li> <li style=3D"margin-bottom: 5px;">Th=
e acquirer&nbsp;<em>licenses the target=E2=80=99s intellectual property:&nb=
sp;</em>The target enters into a nonexclusive license allowing the acquirer=
 to use the target=E2=80=99s ideas, patents, chip designs, etc., for its ow=
n purposes.</li> <li style=3D"margin-bottom: 5px;">In exchange for the waiv=
er (Step 2) and license (Step 3), the acquirer pays the target a big pile o=
f cash.</li> <li style=3D"margin-bottom: 5px;">The target is now more or le=
ss an empty shell. It has lost most of its&nbsp;employees, or at least the =
ones who know about the stuff that made it valuable. It still technically o=
wns its intellectual property =E2=80=94&nbsp;it gave the acquirer a non-exc=
lusive license =E2=80=94&nbsp;but everyone who knows anything about that in=
tellectual property has decamped for the acquirer, so it can=E2=80=99t do a=
nything useful with it. It=E2=80=99s just a collection of miscellany, plus =
the big pile of cash&nbsp;that it got from the acquirer.</li> <li style=3D"=
margin-bottom: 5px;">So the target pays out the cash to its employees. Then=
 maybe it shuts down, or maybe it keeps going in some less ambitious form.<=
/li> </ol> <p style=3D"margin: 16px 0;">This structure is called a =E2=80=
=9Creverse acqui-hire.=E2=80=9D<a href=3D"#footnote-1" style=3D"color: #000=
000; text-decoration: none !important; border-bottom-width: 1px; border-bot=
tom-color: #000000; border-bottom-style: none !important; background-color:=
 transparent !important; display: inline-block;"> <span>[1]</span> </a></p>=
 <p style=3D"margin: 16px 0;">This structure has some disadvantages, compar=
ed to a traditional merger. It is not especially tax-efficient; the target =
pays tax on the licensing fee.<a href=3D"#footnote-2" style=3D"color: #0000=
00; text-decoration: none !important; border-bottom-width: 1px; border-bott=
om-color: #000000; border-bottom-style: none !important; background-color: =
transparent !important; display: inline-block;"> <span>[2]</span> </a>&nbsp=
;Because it leaves the target as an independent company, and gives the acqu=
irer only a nonexclusive license, it is not quite as good as a merger from =
a competitive perspective: What if your biggest competitor manages to strik=
e its own licensing deal with the stub target company?</p> <p style=3D"marg=
in: 16px 0;">But it has some pretty important advantages. For one thing, it=
 probably doesn=E2=80=99t require a vote of the target=E2=80=99s shareholde=
rs. (Just the board of directors.) This allows it to happen more quickly th=
an a merger. It&nbsp;<em>might&nbsp;</em>also allow the reverse acqui-hire =
to happen over the objections of a majority of shareholders, though that=E2=
=80=99s probably not a big factor. (The big shareholders of an early-stage =
AI startup will probably be mostly (1) venture capitalists who are represen=
ted on the board and (2) co-founders and other employees who have some leve=
rage over getting acqui-hired.) But in theory, a reverse acqui-hire that is=
 good for the employees and bad for the outside shareholders might be doabl=
e, whereas a merger that is good for employees and bad for outside sharehol=
ders would be harder to pull off.<a href=3D"#footnote-3" style=3D"color: #0=
00000; text-decoration: none !important; border-bottom-width: 1px; border-b=
ottom-color: #000000; border-bottom-style: none !important; background-colo=
r: transparent !important; display: inline-block;"> <span>[3]</span> </a></=
p> <p style=3D"margin: 16px 0;">The main advantage, though,&nbsp;is probabl=
y antitrust. If it=E2=80=99s not a stock or asset acquisition, it doesn=E2=
=80=99t require an HSR filing, which means you can close the deal before th=
